From 1a75229f39a7f24c4465e246c99b26b19c4011ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 23:46:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(web#12): read back the clean canvas, and keep the
 tile you cannot copy
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The last layer of the dark blink, and the first one that is not a logic error:
under GPU load the weave input's production simply FAILS, per tile, and every
leg 0070-0073 built to catch that failure was failing for the same reason a few
microseconds later. Live telemetry from the fix-stack build on hardware, model
sample under load: 301+ "CPU readback would sample the suppression hole" skips;
"inline-3D recovery draw: n=0 missing=2" on those same frames; "batch weave:
ZERO copies this frame — no submit issued". Meanwhile resolve-dropped = 0,
id-miss = 0, NO-resource = 0. Resolution and matching are healthy. The break is
strictly between "we hold a clean canvas mailbox" and "its pixels are in the
weave input".

David, watching the same page: of the sample's two tiles, the one showing a
glTF alone is STABLE and the one showing a glTF plus a gaussian splat BLINKS.
Per-tile failure rate tracks producer weight. That is not what a broken resource
looks like; it is what a LOST RACE looks like — the heavy producer holds its own
access to the canvas SharedImage longer per frame, so our ProduceSkia /
BeginScopedReadAccess loses far more often on that tile.

WHY THE ZERO-COPY LEG FAILS, AND WHY WE COULD NOT TELL. Every step of that leg —
scratch creation, scratch ProduceSkia, scratch write access, the CANVAS open,
the output snapshot, ProduceOverlayForWeave, the overlay read access, the
overlay type, the WeaveCanvas copy — shared ONE `static bool logged_fail` at
VLOG(1). After the first failure in the process's life, at a verbosity nobody
runs, the whole leg was mute; a capture could say the fallback was being reached
301 times and not say why. Each candidate now has its OWN counter on 0073's
discipline (LOG(ERROR), first 3 then 1-in-300), a distinct reason string, and
the TILE'S IDENTITY (id + rect) in the line — without which the per-tile
correlation above is unmeasurable. The canvas open in particular is split into
its three separate causes, ProduceSkia vs BeginScopedReadAccess vs
CreateSkImage, because "contended" and "gone" are different bugs. The recovery
draw's `missing=N` gains the same breakdown, which is what will confirm on the
next capture that its n=0 has always been the identical contention: it opens the
same resource with the same three calls.

PART 1 — READ BACK FROM THE CLEAN CANVAS, NOT THE PAGE. The CPU-readback leg
sources only from |output|, which since browser#22 is the suppression hole, so
0073 (rightly) gated it shut — leaving the fallback with nothing to sample. But
by the time the zero-copy leg reaches its GPU handoff, the SCRATCH already holds
the finished weave input: the clean canvas SBS, through the quad's UV window,
over the page backdrop, composed from the RESOURCE and never from a page
snapshot. It is hole-free by construction, so 0073's gate does not apply to it.
Every post-compose failure now reads the scratch back and WeavePixels it instead
of falling through — the same pixels the zero-copy leg would have handed over,
taking the slow road. It also un-breaks the branch where the backing exposes no
raw D3D11 texture, which used to be a permanent one-way trip to the gated
|output| readback for the entire process.

PART 2 — KEEP-PREVIOUS, AND A RETRY IN FRONT OF IT. (a) The canvas open retries
ONCE in-frame; a second ask usually wins because the producer has released by
then, and the win is counted so the contention theory stays measurable. (b) When
both attempts lose, the tile stops going dark. The batch weave input is ONE
window-sized texture, written per target at the target's own window position,
and NEVER cleared between frames (the property 0073 discovered while explaining
byte-identical blink captures). So a tile that cannot be copied does not have to
be overwritten: leaving it alone leaves last frame's correct stereo pixels at
the same rect, and re-submitting the rect re-weaves them. New provider entry
point BatchKeepPrevious stages that entry without a copy, guarded on
RECT-UNCHANGED-SINCE-THE-LAST-COPY from the provider's own bookkeeping — the
provider is the only party that knows when the shared texture is reallocated, so
it is the only correct home for the fact (GPU-side DisplayXRTargetState would
have had to mirror a lifetime it cannot observe). At a moved rect it refuses and
we decay to exactly the pre-0074 behaviour. The record is dropped per target by
PruneTarget/PruneTargets (slots are recycled) and wholesale when the shared
texture is reallocated.

Nothing expires a kept tile. Sustained load on one heavy producer means a LONG
consecutive run of failures on that tile, and every frame of it re-reads the
same unchanged record and re-weaves the same pixels; only a real copy, a rect
change, a resize or a retirement moves it. Visually that is a slowed — in the
limit, frozen — turntable on the splat tile while its neighbour runs normally,
which is the correct trade against a black hole. The kept tile is also marked
submitted so the per-tile recovery draw does not paint a MONO left eye over
correctly kept STEREO pixels.

AND THE CLASS THIS CLOSES. When every tile declined, no entry was staged, so
SubmitBatchSync issued no submit at all: the runtime never re-wove, never ran
its first-chunk clear, and 0057's liveness gate then (correctly) skipped the
draw-back — the multi-second silent window that reads from outside as "the weave
is frozen". With keep-previous, a rect-stable tile always stages an entry, so
the batch stays non-empty and the submit keeps going out. Reaching that state
now requires EVERY tile to be both uncopyable AND unkeepable, i.e. the first
frames or a frame where the rects just moved. The ZERO-copies line survives as
the discriminator and now also prints how many tiles were keepable, so if it is
still frequent the log says which of the two it was.

Validated per-TU with clang-cl -fsyntax-only (all three TUs clean; the check
demonstrably bites — it caught two real -Wunsafe-buffer-usage errors in a first
draft, and a static_assert tripwire fails as expected), formatted on changed
line ranges only, and apply-checked against a clean 151 tree with
0070+0071+0072+0073 in front of it.

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-web#12
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
